[Music]  welcome back to cbs this morning  nursing home industry groups say they're  alarmed by a recent coronavirus spike in  their facilities  federal data shows the number of cases  surged in july  after falling in june that's a problem  since more than one quarter of all  covid related deaths in the u.s are  linked to nursing homes  our lead national correspondent david  begno is in miami beach  good morning david good morning you know  the state of florida continues to lead  in the wrong direction  when it comes to the number of people  dying from the coronavirus  yesterday florida set another record for  the number of deaths reported in a  24-hour period  277. when you talk about nursing homes  it's as relevant in florida as anywhere  else there are nearly  700 nursing homes in this state but we  want to begin this morning with a story  out of idaho  where a woman says her elderly mom was  infected with coronavirus inside the  nursing home  and she didn't find out until right  before her mother died we don't know  when my mom got sick  we don't know how long she was sick that  is caroline fary  she says she had no idea her 90-year-old  mother virginia smith  was receiving end-of-life care until two  days before she died  at her boise idaho nursing home one of  her front-line care workers and aide  had called me on my cell phone  from her private cell phone and she  wanted me to be able to talk to my mom  and i didn't understand what that meant  fari says her mother had a high  fever and had lost a tremendous amount  of weight but that the facility where  she lived  shaw mountain of cascadia had not tested  her for covid19  was she treated for coronavirus no  why not good question when she was  finally tested after the family insisted  the results were positive  miss smith died the next day in july  cases and coveted related deaths in  nursing homes  rose along with cases nationally the  trump administration has said protecting  nursing homes is a priority  at this news conference last month  officials promised to provide tests and  rapid testing instruments  to the nation's more than 15 000 nursing  homes but tuesday the president  delivered a much smaller number  we've delivered over 1 800 rapid  point-of-care testing devices to nursing  homes  we're now getting pretty close to the  same numbers of new cases of nursing  homes that we were experiencing at the  highs in the spring  that is mark parkinson ceo of the  american healthcare association  he says the wait times for tests at  nursing homes can take as long as six  days  making it harder to protect the most  vulnerable like virginia smith  whose daughter is heartbroken over how  her mother  spent her last days it's a callous  disregard for my mother and  and for her right to to have a peaceful  death and a dignified death  she suffered miss virginia smith had 12  grandkids 10 great-grandkids her  daughter said she was a devout catholic  who went to mass nearly  every day for 40 years we reached out to  the nursing home in idaho to try and  address some of the concerns that the  family shared with us  but the nursing home has not responded  to our calls we did see that an attorney  who represents the nursing home that  owns the company  gave a statement to an idaho newspaper  which covered this story and i want to  share with you that  that attorney said the company continues  to follow all applicable guidance from  federal and state health agencies  and works to provide self-care and  mitigate the damage  from covet 19. david thank you very much

